  "evidence": "Fetched the raw Wikipedia 'Processing fluency' article directly (en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Processing_fluency&action=raw) rather than relying on a summary. The claim's own supporting quote — 'The Wikipedia article contains NO discussion of conditions where disfluency is preferred' — is factually wrong. The article's Applications-adjacent Research section (paragraph on product design, citing Bloch 1995 'Seeking the Ideal Form: Product Design and Consumer Response,' J. Marketing) states verbatim: 'There is significant evidence that when consumers are presented with multiple choices, they will view objects more positively and more aesthetically pleasing when surrounded by congruent imagery... While consumers enjoy a moderate source of incongruity, too much disorder and unfamiliarity lead to feelings of being overwhelmed.' That is an explicit, named, sourced condition (moderate incongruity/novelty in product form) under which harder-to-process stimuli are preferred over maximally fluent ones — precisely the kind of finding Goal 2 of the research question was searching for ('does disfluency ever read as more preferred... under any documented condition'). So the specific supporting quote used to justify the claim is contradicted by the primary text it purports to describe.\n\nThe other, broader half of the claim — that the article has zero coverage of the aesthetic-usability effect, Kurosu & Kashimura (1995), Don Norman, design rule-breaking, imperfection-in-design, or AI-generated content — does check out: none of those terms, names, or concepts appear anywhere in the raw article (confirmed by direct read of all 96 lines, not just the AI-summarized fetch). So that narrower sub-claim is accurate. But because the claim as submitted rests its 'silent on the question' conclusion partly on the disfluency-preference sub-claim, and that sub-claim is demonstrably false (there is a documented condition, moderate incongruity, sourced to Bloch 1995, where less-fluent stimuli are preferred), the claim overstates what the source says and should not be accepted as written. A corrected version would need to concede the moderate-incongruity/inverted-U nuance rather than asserting a flat zero.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "counterSource": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Processing_fluency (raw wikitext, paragraph citing Bloch, P.H. (1995) 'Seeking the Ideal Form: Product Design and Consumer Response,' Journal of Marketing 59(3):16-29)"
}
